% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 5
% composition-elements: 6/8 metre; dotted quarters, quarter-eighth pairs and dotted halves; rondo form A B A C A (4-bar sections, 20 bars) with an improvised B episode and a written C episode; drone texture (the class holds D and A while a soloist improvises); improvisation within the five-note set D-F-G-A-C; a fermata on the last note
% composition-listen: Ravi Shankar — "Raga Jog", the opening side of the LP Three Ragas (recorded in London, 1956; with Chatur Lal, tabla; reissued on CD by Angel Records, 2000)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ravi+Shankar+Raga+Jog+Three+Ragas+1956
% composition-bridge: Listen for a melody unfolding over a drone, gradual pacing and improvisation that reveals rather than abandons constraints.
% composition-context: A raga is more than a scale, and a Hindustani performance has its own melodic grammar, time cycle and form. This original five-note classroom rondo is not Raga Jog.
% composition-distance: Original equal-tempered pitches and form; no alap phrase, gat, tala pattern or recording passage is copied.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"5-8","role":"soloist over the class drone","prompt":"Choose a three-note path","choices":["rise then rest","circle one note","fall to D"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"17-20","role":"whole class","prompt":"How does A return the last time?","choices":["exactly","ornament one note","softly, then hold"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original instrumental classroom rondo for Grade 5: everyone plays the A theme, one soloist improvises the B episode over the class's drone, everyone reads the written C episode, and A returns between and after them
% dossier-elements: twenty bars in 6/8 (two dotted-quarter beats a bar); D minor pentatonic set D-F-G-A-C, range D4-C5; A theme (bars 1-4, 9-12, 17-20) rises D-F-A, G-A-C in quarter-eighth plus dotted-quarter cells and falls C-A-G-F to a dotted-half D; B episode (bars 5-8) is the class drone, dotted quarters D4 and A4 then a dotted-half D, for a soloist to improvise over; C episode (bars 13-16) opens on a held A4 that leans up to C5, rests for a dotted quarter in bar 14, climbs F-G-A-C and holds A4 so that A can answer; rondo A B A C A; the last D carries a fermata
% dossier-practice: Dotted quarter = 66 is the target; begin at 54. Rehearsal key D minor (no B or E is used, so on Orff barred instruments remove the E and B bars); written range D4-C5, inside the young-unison C4-D5 range for voices on "ah" and inside recorder range. Teach in steps: everyone learns A by ear and then reads it; everyone plays the B drone (D and A, a dotted quarter each); the teacher models a four-bar improvisation over the drone using only D F G A C, then volunteers take turns; read C; put the rondo together, changing soloist each time round.
% dossier-accompaniment: The chord symbol D5 (an open fifth, D and A, no third) is held under the whole piece and played by the page's ballad band, chosen because it is soft and sustained; it is not a tanpura, and the page's drone style (tanpura and tabla) is deliberately not used so that the piece does not imitate Hindustani accompaniment. In class, low bells, a cello or a keyboard can hold D and A instead.
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental piece for recorder, barred percussion, keyboard or voices on a neutral syllable ("ah"); no words, because the bridge is about melody unfolding over a drone. Any words added later should be recorded as a new adaptation.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed range, metre and teaching constraints. Not traditional, not a raga, not a Hindustani composition and not modelled on the reference's melody, alap, gat or tala; the Ravi Shankar recording is a listening bridge only.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, form, drone chord and this ABC encoding. Ravi Shankar's recording of "Raga Jog" remains its owners' copyright and is not included.
% dossier-respect: Name Ravi Shankar as a Hindustani sitarist trained in the Maihar tradition under Allauddin Khan, and name the accompanists on the recording. State explicitly that a five-note set does not define a raga: Jog has its own ascent, descent, characteristic phrases and time of day, and is learned from a teacher. Use the recording and an expert's explanation as the primary sources, and never call this classroom rondo a raga.
% dossier-review: Grade 5 classroom music teacher, Hindustani music educator, second score reader and student playing trial; all reviews still required
