N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; the 8-bar draft was rewritten as a true rondo. Bar-by-bar audit, every bar six eighths: A (bars 1-4, repeated unchanged as bars 9-12 and 17-20) is D quarter, F eighth, A dotted quarter; G quarter, A eighth, C5 dotted quarter; C5 quarter, A eighth, G quarter, F eighth; D4 dotted half (with a fermata in bar 20). B (bars 5-8) is the drone D4 and A4 in dotted quarters three times, then a dotted-half D4. C (bars 13-16) is A4 dotted quarter, C5 quarter, A eighth; G dotted quarter and a dotted-quarter rest; F quarter, G eighth, A quarter, C5 eighth; A4 dotted half. Pitch set D-F-G-A-C, range D4-C5, largest leap the fifth D4-A4 (in the drone, and from the end of A into C) and its reverse from the end of C back to A; the fourth G4-C5 in A; every other move is a step or a third. Form: A B A C A, 4+4+4+4+4 bars. Originality check: the A theme's rising thirds and the C episode were compared with what the reference recording's opening exposes and with common pentatonic children's songs; no shared phrase was found; D minor pentatonic steps and thirds are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 5 soloists can improvise four bars over the drone without losing the 6/8 pulse, whether the class keeps the drone steady under a soloist, and how the ballad band's held D5 sounds can only be settled by a classroom trial. A Hindustani music educator should judge whether the listening framing and the word "drone" are used accurately. The 1956 recording details come from discographies and need checking against an authorized release before publication.
